igned  puzzle game so that brings me to the  next step of this this journey which is  problems and sticking within the digital  games category again I'm gonna show you  a couple of examples of what I think of  as sort of a different class of puzzle  solving games and sometimes these are  called strategy games sometimes they're  categorized under puzzles but things  like infinite factory or factorio which  incidentally both have sort of factory  as their roots because what you're doing  is taking raw materials and assembling  them into machinery that can produce new  materials and then sort of recursively  building up your infrastructure in order  to solve certain challenges a personal  favorite of mine this is a 1993 kind of  version of us The Incredible Machine  which I grew up with is yeah so this is  another example where you're given these  kind of goals in order to  you have to make something happen and  Louisville and you've got your your your  primitive elements or things like our  sorryi things like pulleys and boxing  gloves and fire and all there's all this  stuff that is simulated by the physics  engine but you then assemble together an  elaborate Rube Goldberg machine in order  to accomplish the goal so Marc Brown  again has another great video about this  distinction which I find really useful  wherein he talks about puzzle games in a  different category from problem-solving  games because in puzzle games what  you're doing is more like discovering  the solution and in problem-solving  games you're inventing a solution and  there's of course two words of  difference there Discoverer versus  invent the versus a day so in in a  puzzle solving sort of scenario what  you're doing is essentially I like to  think of puzzle games as a two-player  game between you and the designer it's  just that the designers not there  they've but they've left you little  secret messages in the design of the  level of the puzzle and it's your job  sort of discover those messages put them  together and kind of make sense of what  they intended you to do in this very  delicately handcrafted way whereas in a  in a problem-solving game yeah if the  game designer is giving you a challenge  they've probably solved it themselves  but it might be a much wider space of  possible solutions and they may not have  explored that space very thoroughly and  perhaps they're just waiting for you to  find a more efficient solution than  whatever one that they manage to find  and so there's a lot more of sort of a  process of invention going on in  addition to the bigger solution space  this comes about as well in open world  games like minecraft where you have  materials like redstone which is  essentially a simulation of conductive  material that can be used to assemble  computers and you've also got some just  kind of more literal interpretations of  this idea like T is 100 we're in the  challenges are to literally write code  to a fictional assembly language  in order to solve challenges and there's  also kind of a sandbox mode where some  very industrious people have decided to  reemployment  using this fictional assembly language  so of course the question comes about  you know for if we're giving people  these things that are literally  programming and we're giving them in  giving people games that they enjoy and  are learning how to build robust mental  models of fictional programming language  in a game well couldn't we also use this  to teach computer science or to you know  kind of get people excited about the  idea of programming and there are some  folks in the lab adjacent to mine run by  Tiffany Barnes at NC State the game to  learn lab who have taken that idea  seriously and you know developed a game  called BOTS wherein the whole mechanics  are to write a script for a little robot  to navigate a 3d world in order to  navigate to an exit and you've got the  you've got the actions that the robot  can carry out like movement and picking  things up and you've also got looping  constructs and variables and so on and  you can challenge yourself in terms of  the efficiency of the solution or the  code size of the solution another  example of this that I personally enjoy  is this game called Cube composer  developed by thinking into the  independent developer David Peter which  teaches some functional programming  idioms were in you've got a palette of  possible transformations over on the  left and you drag them over to the right  in a particular sequence to transform a  row of stack of stacks of cubes from an  initial configuration to a goal so that  takes us back to if we zoom out a little  bit back to programs and the question of  whether what we're learning here about  interaction failure mental model  construction can just literally apply to  the process of writing programs in the  wild so at this point I've kind of  started to sketch out a spectrum right  on one end we have puzzles as you would  find in a digital puzzle game which have  a small and very intentionally crafted  solution space  you've got towards the middle of the  spectrum designed problems which could  include problems in a problem-solving  game or perhaps problems in a computer  science class that you might be taking  we're in the solution space is kind of  co-designed with the problem itself like  maybe the designer of the problem  doesn't necessarily have the solution in  mind first and then finally you've got  kind of open programming and programming  in the wild wherein you have a big  solution space nobody has designed to  the problem for you you've just got a  real problem that you have to solve and  it precedes the solution so sort of  inspecting these things as all points  along a spectrum we can sort of start to  identify some correspondences like the  idea that maybe the mechanics of a game  this idea of having a robust mental  model that never changes of the  principles underlying the game on top of  which you can build more problems  corresponds to having a good mental  model of the programming language itself  the puzzles that you can build on top of  that set of ironclad rules are sort of  like the programming problems that arise  within a certain language and then the  puzzle solutions are of course the  programs written in that language that  address the problem and just to draw  another related analogy you can even  think of the whole process itself as  going back to this idea from the  beginning of learning rules from  examples of sort of making designing  abstractions that take particular  examples and extrapolate them to  something a little bit more general so I  actually would like to thank Simon Paton  Jones for giving me this slide so this  was from his morning keynote yesterday  it's a perfect example this was a  beginning programmer who realized that  she could replace a bunch of repetitive  steps in in a program with a simple loop  so this process of abstraction is  exactly the same idea of translating  sort of pattern matching and being able  to identify okay I can represent this in  a more concise way now people have  actually done a lot of research on how  people develop mental models in when  they're  learning how to program I'm not going to  talk a lot about much of this research  is definitely out there and has been  since the seventies it's so people are  still kind of learning about this but  there there was some sort of  controversial research that tried to  conflate the idea of being able to form  robust mental models with the idea that  there are people who fundamentally would  never be able to program which they  later retracted because of course that's  nonsense but the idea of being able to  form robust mental models is still  applicable and so a lot of people have  done some follow-up research on that  which I encourage you to look into if  you're interested now these slides will  be posted after the talk by the way in  the in reality of course most of us are  coding with an incomplete mental model  of our language so if you don't believe  me look up Gary Bernhardt sweat  talk which takes some of our you perhaps  your favorite programming languages and  shows you that the you know the models  that you have of them don't always  correspond to what they actually do so  we're coding with an incomplete mental  model but nonetheless we can get by  right we can still get things done and  in fact most of our bugs come from a  poor alignment between the mental models  of our domains what we're actually  trying to represent and the the program  that's supposed to implement that that  understanding so that finally brings me  to tools for thought so I see okay it's  counting down now okay so so finally I  want to ask this question of like is it  possible for us to build tools that  communicate our mental models directly  to a system so I would argue that in  some ways we actually already do this so  whenever we write tests this is exactly  a way to say here are some examples of  what I think my input output should be  for this function count O's each test is  a communication of the mental model we  also write tests for exactly the same  reason we want mental models to fail  early we want tests to fail so that they  fail now rather than in production type  annotations are another way of being  able to express our mental models to the  system we might take a look at those  exam  and generalize to say hey all the inputs  are strengths so the outputs are its I  would like that to always be the case  and so I will encode that as a part of  my mental model that the system can  check for me  whenever we have fancier types we can  give more precise communication of our  mental models for example if we had a  dependent type system we might be able  to say that the value returned by count  OS should always be a number that's  between zero and the length of a string  we passed in that then brings us to this  concept of like well if we can express  our mental models in this  computationally understandable way can  we use tools to directly run our mental  models as programs and this inspires a  whole field that I like to refer to I  think this is a mean--it oarlocks  language of solver aided tools for  things that you know for things like  program synthesis that can take a  specification a logical description of  what we think our code should do and  just produce the code that actually does  it without us having to write the code  and then check it against a  specification I personally work on a lot  of tools that are fall under the  umbrella procedural generation and you  don't have to read this code but it's a  logical specification of some  constraints on generating a dungeon for  sort of dungeon crawler video game and  you know so it just takes this logical  specification and spits out a playable  game level so these things really  directly let us directly run our mental  models as programs I find that in  practice no because then they get big  and it's hard to tell whether they're  correct  so we've just sort of reduced it to a  new problem we're in we've now created a  very generative system that has a whole  big space of solutions and we need a way  of navigating that space of solutions  and identifying whether or not it's what  we expected it to be and potentially  changing it so finally I'll wrap up with  this idea that I've recently learned  about from David Krakauer which I find a  mentally useful when it comes to talking  about the kinds of systems I would like  to see more of in the world and it's  this concept of cognitive artifacts and  particularly a distinction between  complimentary  and competitive cognitive artifacts so a  cognitive artifact is anything that  helps us think it doesn't have to be a  real physical artifact it could be a  mathematical system like Roman numerals  or multiplication tables or it's a  system of calculus right but it's  something that helps us think and a  competitive cognitive artifact is  something that enhances our mental  abilities as long as we have it with us  but as soon as we discard it it's no  longer we no longer have that ability so  something like a calculator falls under  that category on the other hand a  complementary cognitive artifact is  something that gives us new abilities  but we can actually internalize those  abilities we've gained something new  from using the artifact and therefore  when we discard it we may not be as fast  or as accurate as we were when we had  the artifact but we still have the  ability to we have this mental model and  that's left behind that we can run to  simulate what abilities it gave us in  the first place  another example of this for me was  actually the same artifact but used in  two different ways  GPS navigation as a driver where you're  getting something that's telling you  verbally you know kind of what to do at  every turn  versus when you're using it as a  passenger or somebody walking around  kind of looking at a map and seeing  where you are you get a very different  sense of the geography around you and of  how you fit into kind of a city in a  built environment as a system when you  use these systems this way and in the  case where you're using GPS just to just  to navigate through voice commands  you're not really getting at you're not  getting a coherent map of the place  where you're navigating that you can  then use an apply after the GPS turns  off just again to tie this back into  games  I think augmented reality games like  some of the Niantic games like Pokemon  CO and ingress which preceded it are  even are another example of a  complimentary cognitive artifact in this  space that actually changes our  relationship with the system's were a  part of that can give us a new view and  kind of teach us new things about the  world around us so the takeaways here  and I'm just about out of time are that  automated system  that are really at their best automated  systems are the most awesome when they  help us develop mental models and  facilitate assess human being so that we  can collaborate better with them however  not a whole lot is known about how to do  that so if you are interested in  furthering this research mission I would  definitely invite you to collaborate  with me if anyone is potentially  interested in applying for a PhD we do  have a program for that at an NC State  and I'm also willing to talk with  potential faculty and industry  collaborators so here's my contact  information and thank you very much  [Applause]
 

